# Every generated SVG should be self-describing

This is one area where a couple of very recent agent-oriented GitHub projects have landed on a useful pattern.

`semantic-svg` explicitly separates LLM-directed visual intent from a deterministic compiler that owns coordinates, XML, stable IDs, provenance and validation. [GitHub](https://github.com/AlexAI-MCP/semantic-svg/blob/main/README.md?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

`hyperweave` goes even further and embeds its source spec and hashes back into the generated SVG, so the artifact can be re-ingested and edited later. [GitHub](https://github.com/InnerAura/hyperweave?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

I would copy that idea.

Every exported SVG contains:

```xml
<metadata>
{
  "grim": "ritual.lbrp@1",
  "score": "sha256:...",
  "scene": "temple.basic@2",
  "skin": "stick.publisher@1",
  "source": "liber-o...",
  "compiler": "grimc@0.3.1"
}
</metadata>
```

So if you find the SVG in five years, it knows exactly how it was made.

