# Meshy currently reinforces that distinction

Meshy's programmatic rigging docs currently say their API works best with standard humanoid bipeds and does not support arbitrary body plans reliably. [Meshy Docs](https://docs.meshy.ai/en/api/rigging?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

Their web product is broader—it advertises humanoid and quadruped rigs and a Smart Rig beta for unusual fantasy creatures—but custom skeletons remain less predictable. [Meshy Docs](https://docs.meshy.ai/en/webapp/guides/3d-model/rigging?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

So:

```text
HUMANOID FAMILIAR
    ↓
can demonstrate ritual directly

NON-HUMANOID FAMILIAR
    ↓
guide / narrator / pointer / companion
```

Very clean.

---

# The Meshy preset animations are not the important bit

We can use their:

- idle;
- walk;
- run;
- breathing;
- gestures

for ambient animation.

But **ritual movement must come from GRIM**.

For example, LBRP tracing isn't:

```text
animation clip #438
```

It is:

```yaml
trace:
  geometry: pentagram.earth
  polarity: banishing
  effector: dagger.tip
```

The runtime calculates:

```text
dagger trajectory
       ↓
right-hand target
       ↓
arm IK
       ↓
shoulder/elbow/wrist rotations
```

Therefore a 5' character and an 8' ibis-headed Thoth model execute the same action correctly.

This is the key distinction.

