same skeleton semantics
same poses
same actions

→ hand-drawn SVG Thoth
→ low-poly 3D Thoth
```

If those two can execute the same LBRP from the same Score without modifying the ritual, **we've proven the foundation properly**.

And then, yes, the downstream feature is excellent:

> **Generate your familiar once → it becomes your persistent guide across every grimoire, ritual, spell, meditation and tutorial in the library.**

That is substantially more interesting than simply selling animated grimoires.

Yes — this is the right wedge.

A **personal familiar** that guides you through rituals, speaks to you, has a voice/personality, and can demonstrate actions is much stronger than “just an animated grimoire.”

But for the **foundational stack**, I would **not choose mesh *or* SVG as the canonical thing**.

## Short answer

- **Canonical core:** ritual semantics + score + rig contract
- **Default renderer:** **SVG / 2D**
- **Optional richer renderer:** **GLB/VRM / 3D**
- **Meshy’s role:** avatar-generation tool, **not** the foundation
- **Voice/personality:** separate familiar layer, independent of whether the body is SVG or 3D

So:

> **Build the product SVG-first, with 3D as an optional familiar/avatar mode.**

That is the best lightweight and future-proof approach.

